    "result_text": "Both acceptance tests and the full build pass. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: structured address form for owners\n\n**Contract (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)**\n- Added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (inherited by the `Owner` response via `allOf`).\n- Dropped `address` from the `required` list so a structured-only request is accepted; validity is now enforced as a business rule (address in *either* form + city).\n- Documented `address` as the composed output when structured fields are supplied.\n\n**Address logic (`OwnerAddresses`)** \u2014 kept as the single home for address canonicalization:\n- Added `normalize(OwnerFieldsDto)`: prefers `addressLine1` when non-blank (normalizing each supplied line and setting the flat `address` to the composed value), otherwise normalizes the flat `address` alone \u2014 preserving legacy behaviour.\n- Added `compose(line1, line2)`: `line1`, plus a single space and `line2` when present.\n\n**Pipeline steps** \u2014 reuse the helper, no duplicated logic:\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` (POST) now delegates to `OwnerAddresses.normalize(request)`.\n- `ApplyOwner` (PUT) does the same and copies the new line fields, so the update path handles structured input consistently.\n- `RequireOwnerFields` needed no change: it already checks the resolved `address`, which is non-blank whenever either form is supplied.\n\n**Persistence**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields to the `Owner` entity (auto-mapped by MapStruct in both directions).\n- Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to all four schema files (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\nThe household hash, postcode validation and locality read `city`/`postcode`, which are top-level and identical across both address forms, so they already resolve from the structured fields with no change.\n\nResult: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The structured owner is normalized (`10 king st` \u2192 `10 KING STREET`) with a matching composed `address`, and the flat-address form still works.",
